is, we can set up faster.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.090
And then, by having
the set up faster,

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:35.690
we can do experimenting with
different kinds of setup.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:39.460
So we want to experiment
with Vietnam, for example.

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:44.080
We can do this data modeling,
we can do this Composer.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:48.380
We can do this Kubernetes
cluster is enough or not.

00:18:48.380 --> 00:18:52.960
So we can just express
all the configuration

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.180
using Terraform Plan
and Terraform Apply.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:58.540
And then the projects are there.

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:02.000
We can experiment with it,
and then if we are done,

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:05.980
and we are not satisfied
with the settings,

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:11.560
we can use Terraform Destroy
to delete the projects.

00:19:11.560 --> 00:19:17.750
And we can repeat with
other configurations.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:21.230
And then the other nice thing
is all the core configuration

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:25.610
is stated explicitly in one
file, or probably two files.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:29.450
So we can share
the configuration,

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:31.820
and everybody can see it.

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:34.460
So we can have the
common understandings

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:40.055
about how the settings
of our data warehouse.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:50.830
And then the second part
is, how can we better manage

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:52.880
our user access to data?

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:57.130
So we built a tool
called Data Pier.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:04.600
So Data Pier will assist
us in helping users onboard

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:06.760
with the data warehouse.

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:14.410
So previously, users
requested access to us

00:20:14.410 --> 00:20:20.060
by using email, or coming
to our desk directly.

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.800
It's not scalable
to do that way.

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:23.890
Every time we have--

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:29.500
we get emails to
give them access,

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:31.790
and then every time
we do some work.

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.640
And then some people
just come, and then, hey,

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:37.210
give me give me access
to these data sets.

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:38.920
And then we have
conversation, and then

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.150
yeah, we lost the productivity.

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:48.820
So we built this Data Pier
so people that need access

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:53.560
only open Data Pier, and
then they can request access

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:58.210
in the database dashboard.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:20:59.950
And then in the
Data Pier, people

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:05.410
will also see this
data set can be

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:08.530
accessed by this Google Groups.

00:21:08.530 --> 00:21:11.830
And then they can join
some Google Groups

00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:14.140
that has access to data set.

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.280
So we manage user access
using Google Groups,

00:21:18.280 --> 00:21:25.390
and then we develop the
tools using Google IAM API.

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:28.570
The back and forth, our
tool is Google IAM API.

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:37.540
The third thing that
helps us so very much

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:41.170
to boost our productivity
in developing ELT jobs

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:49.120
is having a tool
that helps people.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.090
They can set their own ELT jobs.

00:21:53.090 --> 00:22:03.050
So we think that SQL
is a very nice thing.

00:22:03.050 --> 00:22:05.630
We can express
transformation in SQL

00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:09.630
because SQL is simple,
straightforward,

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.730
and then it's very intuitive.

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:18.770
So we move from cloud
centered transformation

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:22.220
into SQL-based transformation.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:30.260
So when people know
SQL by doing analysis,

00:22:30.260 --> 00:22:36.420
we can teach them SQL easier
than teaching them how to code.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:46.170
Well, they know SQL, they
can set their own ELT jobs

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:48.230
by using the tools.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:52.880
So people that have
the query only needs

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:56.330
to install our
optimal CLI tools,

00:22:56.330 --> 00:23:01.190
and then submit the query,
SQL query, and then select

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:03.440
what is the destination
data set and table.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:11.100
And then determine, what is
the schedule for the job?

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:15.790
And then the optimal stores
will take care of the rest.

00:23:15.790 --> 00:23:17.720
Everything done automated.

00:23:17.720 --> 00:23:21.430
And then people
will also know, what

00:23:21.430 --> 00:23:23.830
is the result of the execution?

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:32.830
And then the fourth
thing that we built

00:23:32.830 --> 00:23:38.010
is data quality service.

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:44.960
So we have the problems
of convincing people

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:50.400
that this data, this
table, is good to use.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:54.770
And then we don't want
them to always worry

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:58.160
about data quality of the data.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:03.560
We don't want them to be
surprised in solid time,

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.830
because their data has
completeness issues,

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:08.790
has uniqueness issues.

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:12.620
So we built an automated
data quality service

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:20.210
that helps people with checking
the data quality of the--

00:24:20.210 --> 00:24:23.810
yeah-- the quality of data.

00:24:23.810 --> 00:24:28.860
And we implement the
data quality service

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:35.780
in Google products, like
Kubernetes, and then Composer,

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:40.530
to schedule the execution
of data quality profiling.

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:50.460
So when people finish with
creating the ELT jobs,

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:55.080
they can continue with creating
data quality specification.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:25:01.260
Like for these columns, what is
the threshold for completeness?

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.420
What is the threshold
for uniqueness?

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:09.180
So they can submit the
data quality specification

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:14.850
into repos, so the tools
will get the data quality

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:18.330
specification, compiles
it, and then just runs

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:23.180
the data quality profiling,
based on the specification.

00:25:23.180 --> 00:25:28.970
And then the data quality,
data quality assessment

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:35.490
is done immediately after the
execution of its ETL jobs.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:39.710
So people will always know, what
is the quality of their data?

00:25:39.710 --> 00:25:45.980
And then people can get
alert if some columns is not

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:49.780
complete enough, or
some case is not unique.

00:25:54.660 --> 00:26:00.850
And then but still, a problem
with data is still there.

00:26:00.850 --> 00:26:02.850
So what is problem
with data, actually?

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:06.930
So we already know that,
yeah, data warehouse

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:08.500
is data warehouse.

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:11.730
And then if data
comes to us, how

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:17.350
can we create such a
single expression of truth?

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:25.890
How can we create a table,
authoritative table,

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:28.830
that single table can
be used by many people

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:31.720
for analytics, for reporting?

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:37.230
So people don't have to
compare one source of data

00:26:37.230 --> 00:26:40.470
to other source of data,
because, yeah, too many data

00:26:40.470 --> 00:26:43.110
sources to be used.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:47.130
So the other thing
is, how can we

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:51.150
make sure that also the
single version of truth

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.160
is rich in contexts?

00:26:53.160 --> 00:27:00.270
So for example, in GOJEK,
we have booking data

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:05.610
with many dimensions like pick
up, drop off, the duration,

00:27:05.610 --> 00:27:08.320
and the event logs.

00:27:08.320 --> 00:27:13.470
So the other problem is, how
can we structure and model

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.250
the data?

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:20.970
So luckily, BigQuery
has some nice features

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:24.540
that helps us with
such problems, that

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:32.640
solve our problems
with those features.

00:27:32.640 --> 00:27:40.980
And then here, we have event
that shows us, tells us,

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:47.820
how can we structure the data
in our data warehouse using

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:51.960
those features, so we can
get the best of BigQuery

00:27:51.960 --> 00:27:55.420
in solving our problems?

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:56.990
Evan, please come.

