00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.100
of BigQuery or JSON data.

00:29:45.100 --> 00:29:45.600
OK, cool.

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.620
So I'll speed through
this a little bit.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.280
So the constant struggle
is, do we split our data

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:51.540
across many different tables?

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:53.790
Inside of a SQL, what are
you up to do as a BI analyst

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:56.060
to bring all those
back together?

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:57.810
You've got to join
them all back together.

00:29:57.810 --> 00:29:58.950
It's everyone's SQL 101.

00:29:58.950 --> 00:30:00.000
You absolutely hate that stuff.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:01.500
Or you dump it all
on one big table,

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:03.210
and you have data issues.

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:05.920
One fact in one place, not
so much in repeat them.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:08.730
So since you worked with, or
you heard of arrays before,

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.410
one of the really
cool data types

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:13.708
that's natively supported
inside of BigQuery is the array.

00:30:13.708 --> 00:30:15.375
So you have object
oriented programmers.

00:30:15.375 --> 00:30:17.040
They're like, obviously, yeah.

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:18.910
I want to do arrays
for everything.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:21.060
And you have SQL developers
saying, whoa, whoa.

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:22.290
We're rows and columns.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.210
Based keep your
arrays to yourself.

00:30:24.210 --> 00:30:26.490
BigQuery as a data warehouse,
as a data platform,

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:28.110
can support both,
and we're going

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:29.790
to walk through
examples of what's

00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:33.120
really beneficial of storing
your data nested in a repeated

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:33.810
format.

00:30:33.810 --> 00:30:35.040
This is GOJEK.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:38.110
Zaki and Gaurav provided
me with an awesome table.

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:39.510
This is a bookings table.

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:43.530
So Zaki mentioned you have
lots of different information

00:30:43.530 --> 00:30:47.580
about GOJEK bookings, like
motorcycle ride shares, that

00:30:47.580 --> 00:30:49.260
are all inside of one table now.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:51.570
All that different context
from 15 different tables

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:53.150
is all in one.

00:30:53.150 --> 00:30:55.980
So if you notice, it
looks really strange

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:59.290
if you've never seen arrays
inside a BigQuery before.

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:01.830
So this is actually
only four rows

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:03.280
of data on this screenshot.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:06.023
The others, 1,000 or
so, totally in BigQuery.

00:31:06.023 --> 00:31:07.440
But what's interesting
is that you

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:10.650
see that there's a status field
and a time field for arrays

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:13.800
that look like there are
four rows of data inside

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:15.120
of that first one.

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:16.495
That's an array of data.

00:31:16.495 --> 00:31:18.870
So if you're familiar with
like, arrays it's the bracket.

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:22.060
Essentially, BigQuery visually
breaks that out for you.

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:23.610
But technically,
behind the scenes,

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:25.210
it's still in those brackets.

00:31:25.210 --> 00:31:26.250
And it's going to get
really interesting

00:31:26.250 --> 00:31:28.290
when you see the demo of
how to work with that.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:30.450
It natively supports that,
that's one data type,

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:32.350
and you'll see how we work
with those in just a minute.

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:33.810
The other data type
that's interesting,

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:35.685
if you're looking at
the top and you're like,

00:31:35.685 --> 00:31:37.680
I'm familiar in
SQL with an alias,

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:39.570
like naming a field
something else.

00:31:39.570 --> 00:31:43.350
What on earth is events
dot something, pickup dot

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.330
something, destination
dot something?

00:31:45.330 --> 00:31:48.420
It almost looks like you've got
six or four different tables

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:50.110
all inside of one.

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:52.480
The structure-- and this is
not specific to BigQuery.

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:54.110
This is just
generally part of SQL.

00:31:54.110 --> 00:31:55.340
It's supported in BigQuery.

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:58.260
Is called a STRUCT,
short for structure.

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:00.180
You combine both
of them together,

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:02.130
and you can have
really wide tables.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.620
A STRUCT is kind of
like a pre-joined table

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:05.730
inside of BigQuery.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:10.050
So you both get the width of
having information on events,

00:32:10.050 --> 00:32:11.313
pick up, destination.

00:32:11.313 --> 00:32:13.230
I'm going to show you
an example data set that

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:15.210
has over 30 STRUCTS in it.

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:18.150
That's the Google
Analytics schema.

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:21.480
And you get the granularity
of having one row technically

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:24.102
represents one order,
like a ride share pickup

00:32:24.102 --> 00:32:25.060
or something like that.

00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:28.170
But within one row, you can
have extreme granularity

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:30.750
at the array level, with as
many different things happening

00:32:30.750 --> 00:32:31.860
with one order.

00:32:31.860 --> 00:32:33.990
So your queries can
get at the high level.

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:35.460
Count star from all orders?

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:36.660
Sure, I got four orders.

00:32:36.660 --> 00:32:39.300
Or I can look deep
into the array values

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:43.410
there, without having to worry
about data stored in a repeated

00:32:43.410 --> 00:32:45.838
fashion, like a
truly denormalized.

00:32:45.838 --> 00:32:47.880
All right, so a little
bit of a practice for you,

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:49.770
and a question I'm a technical
[INAUDIBLE] developer.

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:51.150
You're not going to
leave you without a quiz,

00:32:51.150 --> 00:32:53.820
and we definitely have some
cool lab promotions for you,

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:54.398
as well.

00:32:54.398 --> 00:32:56.940
In a schema inside of BigQuery,
if you work with the BigQuery

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.750
before, you've seen this before.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:03.590
Yell out maybe one or a
couple of the STRUCTS.

00:33:03.590 --> 00:33:05.390
How do you know
whether it's a STRUCT?

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:07.565
You're looking for a
data type of record.

00:33:07.565 --> 00:33:09.440
Whenever you see record
in somebody's schema,

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:11.440
you're like, whoa, this
