00:33:33.970 --> 00:33:36.130
That's another argument
for a different time.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:37.630
Ingesting that
in, you definitely

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:39.520
get some nested and
repeated values.

00:33:39.520 --> 00:33:43.255
You can actually nest
values 15 layers deep.

00:33:43.255 --> 00:33:44.380
The computer can handle it.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:45.797
Mentally a human,
if you gave them

00:33:45.797 --> 00:33:48.170
15 essentially tabs
and a data set,

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:50.078
you're BI analyst
might get really mad.

00:33:50.078 --> 00:33:51.620
All right, see,
you're exactly right.

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:54.160
These are essentially kind
of like four other tables

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:55.600
that are pre-joined into there.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.030
Pre-joined, you can think for
a huge performance benefit,

00:33:58.030 --> 00:34:00.520
means that you actually can
get a lot of performance not

00:34:00.520 --> 00:34:03.700
having to run that join behind
the scenes, which is cool.

00:34:03.700 --> 00:34:06.530
So MySQL, Postgres cloud
SQL, that's transactional.

00:34:06.530 --> 00:34:08.650
That's 80% percent
writes, 20% reads.

00:34:08.650 --> 00:34:12.429
BigQuery, data warehouse,
you're doing the majority,

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:14.650
80% reads, instead of writes.

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:17.409
It's better to have that data
stored inside of one table,

00:34:17.409 --> 00:34:20.980
not doing performance
hits on joins.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:21.909
All right.

00:34:21.909 --> 00:34:30.310
Now, look at the mode, and then
find me the array or arrays.

00:34:30.310 --> 00:34:31.659
You guys are fast.

00:34:31.659 --> 00:34:32.920
That's exactly right.

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:36.310
The events have that double
level of granularity.

00:34:36.310 --> 00:34:37.989
The rows of this table, yes.

00:34:37.989 --> 00:34:40.830
They are on the order level.

00:34:40.830 --> 00:34:42.340
When you look at
Google Analytics,

00:34:42.340 --> 00:34:43.570
it's on this session level.

00:34:43.570 --> 00:34:45.429
But within each of
those different rows,

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:47.110
you can get an
extreme granularity

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:50.050
by nesting an array,
which is great.

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:52.233
But when you write
SQL against an array,

00:34:52.233 --> 00:34:53.650
unless you're
experienced with it,

00:34:53.650 --> 00:34:55.233
which you're going
to see in the demo,

00:34:55.233 --> 00:34:57.620
