00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:20.950
but all that means is that this
person right here is an array.

00:38:20.950 --> 00:38:23.950
And we need to essentially
unpack that array

00:38:23.950 --> 00:38:26.343
back into rows and columns
before we can operate on it.

00:38:26.343 --> 00:38:28.510
BigQuery will definitely
take your array, ingest it,

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:29.660
no problems.

00:38:29.660 --> 00:38:32.260
But before we do that,
let's talk about how arrays

00:38:32.260 --> 00:38:33.520
work inside of BigQuery.

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:35.020
If you're familiar with
object oriented programming,

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.500
you're going to
see arrays a lot.

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:38.200
And if you're a data
analyst, data engineer,

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:40.700
you're definitely the see them
a lot in your data warehouse.

00:38:40.700 --> 00:38:42.280
An array is an
ordered set of values.

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:45.130
They must share a data type.

00:38:45.130 --> 00:38:47.740
So here's just an array
of strings, no big deal.

00:38:47.740 --> 00:38:49.227
We just passed in
a bunch of fruit,

00:38:49.227 --> 00:38:50.310
and stored it as an array.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:53.650
And we're specifying
an array here.

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:54.580
And we got one row.

00:38:54.580 --> 00:38:57.690
We got four things in that row.

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.040
Now can also infer it.

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:02.578
So I don't tell it that
this is a string value,

00:39:02.578 --> 00:39:04.370
but it automatically
just knows that that's

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:05.520
going to be an array.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:08.150
What are some cool things
that you can do with arrays?

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:09.800
Once it's in an
array format, you

00:39:09.800 --> 00:39:13.400
want to particularly say, hey, I
want to find the second element

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:14.002
in the array.

00:39:14.002 --> 00:39:15.460
Who can tell me,
who's brave enough

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:19.048
to shout it out and have it
be recorded on YouTube, that--

00:39:19.048 --> 00:39:21.590
what is it, what are we going
to return here when I run this?

00:39:21.590 --> 00:39:25.160
I've got raspberry,
blackberry, strawberry, cherry,

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:27.200
and I've got offset
of two in there.

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:29.870
Look on line 41 one
before you shout out.

00:39:29.870 --> 00:39:30.620
What do you think?

00:39:30.620 --> 00:39:32.690
Hands up if you think cherry.

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:35.000
Hands up if you think raspberry.

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:37.180
What about blackberry?

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:38.210
You all are too smart.

00:39:38.210 --> 00:39:39.643
What about strawberry?

00:39:39.643 --> 00:39:40.310
All right, yeah.

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:46.850
You definitely are earning
your alcoholic beverages later.

00:39:46.850 --> 00:39:48.170
It is zero indexed.

00:39:48.170 --> 00:39:49.580
Yes, exactly.

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:52.735
So if you didn't wanted to
offset, you could do ordinal.

00:39:52.735 --> 00:39:53.860
How about now, yell it out.

00:39:53.860 --> 00:39:55.444
What we're going to return now?

00:39:55.444 --> 00:39:56.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:57.430
