This is the same thing here.

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:28.030
I can't run a WHERE clause
against a typical WHERE clause,

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:29.790
select star from where,
against this item.

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:32.040
They're on two different
levels of granularity, right?

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:33.722
How do I work with arrays?

00:40:33.722 --> 00:40:35.430
Well, let's go back
to the GOJEK example.

00:40:38.482 --> 00:40:39.940
Taking a look at
the preview, we're

00:40:39.940 --> 00:40:42.480
going to look at
the payment method.

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:44.850
One insight, always start
with a good question, right?

00:40:44.850 --> 00:40:47.880
All data analysis begins
with a good question.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.470
Cash, cash, cash.

00:40:49.470 --> 00:40:52.350
Is cash the most popular
method for the orders?

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:54.965
Well we can write a very
simple single SQL query.

00:40:54.965 --> 00:40:57.090
We're just going to say
count orders by the payment

00:40:57.090 --> 00:41:00.988
method, group it by-- we're not
even touching the array values.

00:41:00.988 --> 00:41:02.530
And you get the
answer back for that.

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:03.447
You're like, OK, cool.

00:41:03.447 --> 00:41:05.350
This Is basic SQL we
can see as an insight.

00:41:05.350 --> 00:41:08.400
Most of the people in this
demo table pay with cash,

00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:10.180
and then the second
highest is Go-Pay,

00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:12.097
the native app based payment.

00:41:12.097 --> 00:41:12.680
Not a problem.

00:41:12.680 --> 00:41:14.187
We're not touching
any array values.

00:41:14.187 --> 00:41:16.270
All right, well I'm going
to go back to the table,

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:18.670
and there's a bunch of
interesting status fields

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:20.410
associated with an order--

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:23.191
driver canceled,
created, driver found.

00:41:25.443 --> 00:41:27.610
If you're familiar with
SQL, one of the easiest ways

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:30.520
you can get a distinct list
of values is select distinct.

00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:32.233
So select distinct status.

00:41:32.233 --> 00:41:34.150
If someone comes along
to you with this query,

00:41:34.150 --> 00:41:35.110
you might just
look at it and go,

00:41:35.110 --> 00:41:36.340
that's absolutely
going to run fine.

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:37.882
Boom, you're going
to get this error.

00:41:37.882 --> 00:41:39.478
Immediately you're
going to get angry,

00:41:39.478 --> 00:41:40.520
and don't worry about it.

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:41.937
But I want you to
train your mind.

00:41:41.937 --> 00:41:49.310
As soon as you see array, I
want you to memorize this word--

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:51.170
UNNEST.

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:54.440
If you ever get an array
and you need to unpack it,

00:41:54.440 --> 00:42:00.220
the fancy way that we decided
to call it is UNNEST that array.

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:04.620
So once we break
that array apart,

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:07.840
it's going to look like this.

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:08.980
Normal SQL runs fine.

00:42:08.980 --> 00:42:11.440
You can see that
there are, let's see,

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:13.310
nine unique statuses.

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:15.100
This doesn't give
us too much context.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:17.980
Let's add an additional
aggregated field.

00:42:17.980 --> 00:42:20.860
Let's just say, hey, let's do
a count of the orders buying

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:24.790
each of the distinct
statuses that were in there.

00:42:24.790 --> 00:42:27.240
Now a note.

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:29.190
Let's do a pause here.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:32.370
So what you've done--
event is normally

00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:35.520
stored like this, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:42:35.520 --> 00:42:38.790
That's a normal array, nested.

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:42.980
Unnested means we're
going to break this apart.

00:42:42.980 --> 00:42:45.080
That's UNNEST.

00:42:45.080 --> 00:42:47.420
Now one really devilish thing
you can do in your code,

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:52.260
and I don't recommend
it, is essentially--

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:56.160
this in my mind, conceptually
is very different--

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:59.040
this nested, this unnested.

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:00.060
Do me a huge favor.

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:03.480
When you UNNEST your arrays,
AKA turn them from line 106

00:43:03.480 --> 00:43:06.450
to the rest, don't give
them the same alias.

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.642
It's incredibly confusing.

00:43:08.642 --> 00:43:10.350
And one of the best
ways you can help out

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:13.880
your fellow programmers,
unnested_array, you

00:43:13.880 --> 00:43:15.130
can name it whatever you want.

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:17.370
Just make it really
obvious which one is which,

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:18.900
because that's the
one you actually

00:43:18.900 --> 00:43:21.300
have as your alias when
you're pulling it from there.

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:23.050
And you can see that
that works just fine.

00:43:23.050 --> 00:43:26.070
Let's see the total
amount of status.

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:28.080
If I'm going to rename
it on the fly for a demo,

00:43:28.080 --> 00:43:30.498
I need to rename
it in all places.

00:43:30.498 --> 00:43:32.040
So that's the note
that I have there,

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:35.290
always rename it as
something different.

00:43:35.290 --> 00:43:37.040
And this is fundamentally
what's going on.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:39.350
Nested, unnested.

00:43:39.350 --> 00:43:40.970
Rows.

00:43:40.970 --> 00:43:42.105
This is all in one row.

00:43:42.105 --> 00:43:44.105
This is all in normal,
regular rows and columns,

00:43:44.105 --> 00:43:45.985
and you can go crazy
with SQL on it.

00:43:45.985 --> 00:43:47.360
So you can see
the majority here.

00:43:47.360 --> 00:43:50.930
We just have the quick
insight, majority of orders

00:43:50.930 --> 00:43:53.570
have the status driver found.

00:43:53.570 --> 00:43:56.282
And then created, picked_up,
completed, customer cancelled.

00:43:56.282 --> 00:43:57.740
Link it to a
dashboard if you want.

00:43:57.740 --> 00:43:59.840
You can actually explore
directly in Data Studio.

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:01.548
I'm not going to show
you that right now,

00:44:01.548 --> 00:44:04.520
but you can link a table
directly into Data Studio.

00:44:04.520 --> 00:44:05.480
All right.

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:07.860
I do want to leave a little
bit of time at the end.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:09.230
So one of the things
you might be asking

00:44:09.230 --> 00:44:10.730
is when you get
access to this code.

00:44:10.730 --> 00:44:12.663
Hey Evan, I don't have
a gojek.booking table

00:44:12.663 --> 00:44:13.580
because it's a sample.

00:44:13.580 --> 00:44:17.100
But what you do have is you have
the Google Analytics schema.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:20.390
And if you think two or three
STRUCTS inside of a table

00:44:20.390 --> 00:44:22.390
is a lot, if you--

00:44:22.390 --> 00:44:24.140
and this is all into
BigQuery public data.

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:27.630
There's over 130 BigQuery public
data sets for you to play with.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:30.500
One of them, or if you have your
own Google Analytics account,

00:44:30.500 --> 00:44:31.833
is this Google Analytics sample.

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:37.450
Any guesses on how many-- and
you might have already seen

00:44:37.450 --> 00:44:38.080
the answer--

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:40.692
how many STRUCTS are in the
Google Analytics schema?

00:44:40.692 --> 00:44:41.650
How wide is the schema?

00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:43.220
Just throw out a number.

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:46.150
One, two, three?

00:44:46.150 --> 00:44:47.160
Seven?

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:47.703
Let's see.

00:44:47.703 --> 00:44:49.120
What we're are we
going to search?

00:44:49.120 --> 00:44:50.440
Oh, it's already cheated.

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:54.870
What word are you going
to search for the type?

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:57.400
You're going to
search for record.

00:44:57.400 --> 00:45:00.940
There's 32 STRUCTS inside
the Google Analytics scheme,

00:45:00.940 --> 00:45:03.310
essentially 32
pieces of context.

00:45:03.310 --> 00:45:05.070
Other tables that
are jammed in there.

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:06.100
That's because
there's a lot of data

00:45:06.100 --> 00:45:07.558
that's collected
for your visitors,

00:45:07.558 --> 00:45:08.970
for your e-commerce website.

00:45:08.970 --> 00:45:11.770
And you can see you
can even nest STRUCTS.

00:45:11.770 --> 00:45:15.190
hits.product.isimpression.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:18.590
hits.eventinfo, event category.

00:45:18.590 --> 00:45:20.230
So you can get a
really, really, what's

00:45:20.230 --> 00:45:22.900
called denormalized,
or gigantic, singular

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:24.340
table for reporting insights.

00:45:24.340 --> 00:45:26.482
And that's exactly how
Google Analytics is set up.

00:45:26.482 --> 00:45:28.690
And that's the data set that
you can experiment with.

00:45:28.690 --> 00:45:31.880
You can actually, as I said
before, you can go 15 deep.

00:45:31.880 --> 00:45:38.070
So if you wanted to fix
this query, for example,

00:45:38.070 --> 00:45:46.740
page title is [? or ?] array.

00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:52.138
What you would need to do is
hits, you're unnesting that.

00:45:52.138 --> 00:45:54.430
Give it a different name,
don't name it the same thing,

00:45:54.430 --> 00:45:55.865
don't confuse everybody.

00:45:55.865 --> 00:45:57.615
And then you'll be
able to select from it,

00:45:57.615 --> 00:46:00.580
and you get some really
cool data that's returned.

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:02.765
So why are-- a lot of
you might be wondering,

00:46:02.765 --> 00:46:05.140
I'm never going to come across
nested [INAUDIBLE] fields.

00:46:05.140 --> 00:46:06.310
I don't have any JSON data.

00:46:06.310 --> 00:46:08.630
Ha-ha, you will soon.

00:46:08.630 --> 00:46:11.890
Because if you're building
a classification model

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:13.450
inside of BigQuery,
which now you

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:15.580
can do with just
two lines of code,

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:18.100
create model, model
type classification,

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:19.290
using logistic regression.

00:46:19.290 --> 00:46:22.370
A little bit outside
